Here is code that will produce an example data frame:

df = data.frame(AA = sample(1:10, 10), BB = sample(1:10,10), CC =
sample(1:10,10), DD=sample(1:10,10))

... where the column names are the sample type, and under each sample
are numbers representing protein abundances.

This data frame may be associated with tags for each sample:

tags <- c("Type 1", "Type 2", "Type 1", "Type 2")

... which should line up with each column.

In the end, I settled for a much less efficient data frame which works
well with ggplot2:

new.df = cbind(numeric(0),character(0),character(0))
for (i in 1:NCOL(df)) {
+ new.df <- rbind(new.df,cbind(df[,i],colnames(df)[i],tags[i])) }

This works well enough for most samples, but many of the samples the
program needs to handle are extremely large.

I was trying to find a way to eliminate the repetition of the tags and
column names in the final data frame, but was unable to find a way to
do so.

I am unfamiliar with Bioconductor, and do not yet feel comfortable
enough with R to implement a more efficient data structure.  I am
actually an intern who just started working here (and learning R) two
weeks ago.

Andrie and Hadley - thank you for your quick responses and the effort
you put into trying to help me.  I think the current implementation
will work well enough.

On Jun 11, 7:35 am, Hadley Wickham <had...@rice.edu> wrote:
> > Usually, I want a graph where the 'x' data is by sample, and the 'y'
> > data is by number in that column.  (Picture a boxplot with the x data
> > segmented by sample.)  Is it possible to use a data frame such that
> > there is a column of sample names and a column of lists of numbers
> > (abundances of the different proteins) with ggplot2?  Or would I need
> > to create a 2 column data table where the sample name is replicated
> > for each protein abundance (which would seem to be a much less
> > efficient method).
>
> Basically the question comes down to efficiency.  If you have very
> large datasets, it may be more efficient to store your data in a
> non-standard format.  However, it's not an efficient use of my time to
> figure out how to get the grammar working with any sort of data
> structure one can dream up.  ggplot2 demands a standard format which
> in my data analysis experience is very close to what you should be
> using anyway.
>
> If you are concerned about efficiency, the best solution (although
> difficult to do in R) is to implement a data structure that acts like
> a data frame but uses a different implementation to speed up
> operations that are inefficiency for your data type.  Bioconductor may
> already have tools to do this.
>
> Hadley
